-- title:
--   Germ-pinned function-field embedding of a curve model over a completion
-- statement:
--   Fix a commutative ring $R$, a valuation subring $A_2$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}=$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, and a field $\bar F$ that is an $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra. Let $\pi_X : X \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ be a morphism of schemes and $\bar s : \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q} \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a point. Let $\mathfrak M$ be a curve model of $\bar F$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, that is: an integral scheme $\mathfrak M.C$ with a proper, smooth morphism $\mathfrak M.\mathrm{toBase}$ of relative dimension $1$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a ring isomorphism $\mathrm{ffEquiv} : \bar F \cong K(\mathfrak M.C)$ compatible with the structural map $\overline{\mathbb Q} \to K(\mathfrak M.C)$, a bijection from the closed points of $\mathfrak M.C$ onto the places of $\bar F/\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (valuation subrings of $\bar F$, proper, containing $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, with principal ideals) matching stalks with valuation subrings, and the property that every finite set of points lies in one affine open. Assume an isomorphism $e_{\mathfrak M} : \mathfrak M.C \to X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q}$, i.e. $e_{\mathfrak M}$ followed by the second projection is $\mathfrak M.\mathrm{toBase}$. Let $\mathcal O_0$ be a commutative ring that is an algebra over $\mathrm{ratClosure}\,A_2$, the topological closure of the prime subfield inside the completion $\widehat C := A_2.\mathrm{valuation.Completion}$, and let $f_0 : \mathcal X_0 \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ be a morphism whose base change $\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C}$ along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathcal O_0 \to \mathrm{ratClosure}\,A_2 \to \widehat C$ is integral. Finally assume an isomorphism $u$ from $X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} \operatorname{Spec}\widehat C$ (the fibre product of $\pi_X$ with $\operatorname{Spec}\widehat C \to \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q} \xrightarrow{\bar s} \operatorname{Spec} R$) to $\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C}$, commuting with the projections to $\operatorname{Spec}\widehat C$. Write $\varphi : \mathcal X_{0,\widehat C} \to \mathfrak M.C$ for $u^{-1}$ followed by the canonical map $X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R}\operatorname{Spec}\widehat C \to X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R}\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and then by $e_{\mathfrak M}^{-1}$. Then there is a ring homomorphism $j : \bar F \to K(\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C})$ with four properties: (i) $j$ is pull-back of rational functions along $\varphi$, read on germs: for every $x \in \bar F$, every open $U \subseteq \mathfrak M.C$ containing the generic point and with $\varphi^{-1}U$ containing the generic point of $\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C}$, and every section $s$ over $U$ whose germ at the generic point is $\mathrm{ffEquiv}(x)$, the germ of $\varphi^{*}s$ at the generic point of $\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C}$ equals $j(x)$; (ii) on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $j$ agrees with the structural homomorphism $\widehat C \to K(\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C})$ induced by the projection $\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C} \to \operatorname{Spec}\widehat C$, precomposed with $\overline{\mathbb Q} \hookrightarrow \widehat C$; (iii) the subfield generated by the image of $\widehat C$ together with the image of $j$ is all of $K(\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C})$; (iv) for any finite subset $t \subseteq \bar F$ whose elements are $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-linearly independent and any family $c : t \to \widehat C$, vanishing of $\sum_{x \in t} c(x)\, j(x)$ (with $c(x)$ viewed in $K(\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C})$) forces $c \equiv 0$.
--
--   This is the comparison, at the level of function fields, between the geometric fibre $X_{\overline{\mathbb Q}}$ presented as a curve model with prescribed places and the generic fibre over the completion $\widehat C$ of a second model $\mathcal X_0 \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$: the pull-back map $j$ is pinned on germs, its image together with $\widehat C$ generates, and $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-independence is preserved, so that $\bar F$ and $\widehat C$ are linearly disjoint in $K(\mathcal X_{0,\widehat C})$. It is the germ-level input to the two Čerednik–Drinfeld statements [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_of_iso_pullback_completion_one_zero_of_two_mul_dvd`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_of_iso_pullback_completion_one_zero_of_two_mul_dvd) and [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_of_iso_pullback_completion_zero_one_of_two_mul_dvd`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_of_iso_pullback_completion_zero_one_of_two_mul_dvd), and relies on [`AlgebraicGeometry.functionField_pullback_generates_and_linearIndependent`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.functionField_pullback_generates_and_linearIndependent) together with [`AlgebraicGeometry.genericPoint_mem_preimage_comp_pullback_fst_of_injective_algebraMap`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.genericPoint_mem_preimage_comp_pullback_fst_of_injective_algebraMap).
